 "checked_text": "this page answers how congwin grows, then drops when loss happens. i'll use the rules here with an example threshold of 8 mss, starting at the slow start rule.\nexample: ssthresh=8\n\nin slow start, each ack adds 1 mss, so across a whole round trip the window doubles. from 1 mss, our example goes 1 to 2 to 4 to 8, stopping when it hits the threshold.\ncwnd 1 -> 2 -> 4 -> 8\nuntil threshold\n\nonce congwin reaches the threshold, tcp switches to congestion avoidance. now growth is gentler: about +1 mss per rtt, so 8 becomes 9, then 10, 11, and 12.\ncwnd 8 -> 9 -> 10 -> 11 -> 12\nafter that\n\nat round 8 in our example, congwin is 12 mss and a timeout occurs. the multiplicative decrease rule halves the threshold to 6, but resets congwin all the way to 1 mss.\n12/2=6; cwnd=1\n\nafter that reset, tcp starts slow start again, now aiming for the new threshold 6. so the next rounds go 1 to 2 to 4 to 6, capped at the new ssthresh.\nrestart ss\nnew ssthresh=6\nafter timeout: 1 -> 2 -> 4 -> 6\n\nso, in this example, congwin is 1, 2, 4, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, then timeout resets it to 1. big idea: exponential growth first, linear growth next, sharp cut on timeout.\n1,2,4,8,9,10,11,12,1\nstart cwnd 1\nslow start\nhit ssthresh\nadditive increase\ntimeout\nreset cwnd 1\nstart cwnd 1 -> slow start\nslow start -> hit ssthresh\nhit ssthresh -> additive increase\nadditive increase -> timeout\ntimeout -> reset cwnd 1"
